    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1243">

                <pb facs="1134" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/2151</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of the Navy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Roosevelt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-10">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 10, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your letter of May 7, 1915,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1243-f1"/>
                    enclosing a copy of a personal note addressed to the Secretary of State by the
                    British Ambassador at this Capital, in the last paragraph of which he refers to
                    the action of the captain of the United States ship <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Iroquois</hi> in 1861, when he reported to the Secretary of the Navy in his
                    letters of November 17, November 23, and November 25, 1861, that he had made
                    arrangements to be informed of the movements of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sumter</hi> in the port of Martinique.</p>
                <p>It is noted that the Department of State will be gratified if it may be furnished
                    at the earliest moment with copies of the correspondence above mentioned.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to enclose herewith, from the original documents, copies of the
                    letters, as requested.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1243-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Copies of these letters are to be found in the <hi rendition="#italic">Official
                        Records of the Union and Confederate Navies in the War of the
                    Rebellion</hi>, Series I, Volume I, Operations of the cruisers, January 19,
                    1861, to December 31, 1862, on pages 208, 209, 212, 213, 214.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Franklin D. Roosevelt</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
